  <h1>7. University Viva Voce Examination Cheat Sheet</h1>
  <div class="viva-q">Q1: What is the primary difference between Program 1 (Vanilla JS) and Program 2 (React)?</div>
  <div class="viva-a">Program 1 uses imperative DOM manipulation (document.createElement) to manually alter elements in the browser tree. Program 2 uses React's declarative Virtual DOM model (UI = f(state)) where state updates trigger efficient reconciliation diffs.</div>

  <div class="viva-q">Q2: How does Program 3 handle POST requests without Express?</div>
  <div class="viva-a">The incoming request is a Readable Stream. We buffer binary chunks with req.on("data", chunk => ...) and parse the combined string into an object using JSON.parse() on req.on("end").</div>

  <div class="viva-q">Q3: Why use bcrypt over simple SHA-256 for password security?</div>
  <div class="viva-a">SHA-256 is fast and susceptible to GPU brute-forcing and precomputed rainbow tables. bcrypt incorporates an adaptive work factor (salt rounds = 10) and random salting, making brute-force attacks computationally infeasible.</div>

  <div class="viva-q">Q4: What role does JWT play in Program 4 and 5?</div>
  <div class="viva-a">JWT enables stateless authentication. After login, the server issues a signed cryptographic token that the client transmits in the Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt; header, eliminating the need for server-side session stores.</div>

  <div class="viva-q">Q5: Why is CORS necessary in Program 5?</div>
  <div class="viva-a">The React frontend runs on port 5173 while the Express backend runs on port 5005. Because the origins differ, browsers block cross-origin requests unless the server sends Access-Control-Allow-Origin headers via the cors() middleware.</div>

